package determinism

// legacy_process_named_funcs.go — the DRAINING LEDGER for TestNoNewProcessNamedTestFunctions.
//
// It exists for the same reason legacy_process_named_list.go did, one level down. When the FILE freeze
// landed, 158 files were renamed in a single batch and that ledger drained to empty in the same commit.
// The function names could not be treated the same way: renaming 439 functions touches the `-run`
// regexes in the Makefile, the parallel e2e runner and the docs, so doing it as a precondition would
// have blocked the freeze behind a large mechanical change — the exact sequencing S3 §2 裁决 6 rejected
// for filenames ("止血 first, the renames are optional cleanup").
//
// So this one starts full and drains over time. The rules that keep it from rotting are the same two:
//
//   - an entry is only ever REMOVED, never added, once the freeze is in place — enforced by
//     legacyFuncLedgerCap in test_naming_test.go, not merely asserted here; and
//   - TestNoNewProcessNamedTestFunctions FAILS on an entry that no longer names an existing function,
//     so a rename that forgets to delete its line turns the gate red in the same commit.
//
// The second rule is what makes this a ledger rather than an exemption list. An allow-list that is only
// ever appended to eventually permits everything, which is the failure mode of every such list that
// does not check itself.

// KEYED "<path>: <FuncName>", NOT BY BARE NAME
// -------------------------------------------
// origin: line-2 external review M7 / PI-7. Keying on the bare name made every entry a REUSABLE pass:
// a new file anywhere in the tree containing `func TestB1ClusterVerdict` — a name already listed —
// walked straight through the gate. That is a category-scoped exemption, which is exactly the shape
// this repo's own rule forbids: an exemption covers the sites that were examined, never future ones.
//
// ON THE NUMBERS 436 AND 439
// --------------------------
// The line-2 plan estimated 529 from a hand-written grep. The name-keyed ledger held 436. The
// site-keyed one holds 439, and the difference is the second half of M7: three names each exist in TWO
// packages —
//
//	TestD7Matrix   test/d7/integration_test.go   and   test/e2e/all_phases_test.go
//	TestD8Matrix   test/d8/integration_test.go   and   test/e2e/all_phases_test.go
//	TestD9Matrix   test/d9/cutover_integration_test.go and test/e2e/all_phases_test.go
//
// — and the name-keyed map collapsed each pair into one entry. So the old ledger was not just permissive
// about future sites; it was UNDERCOUNTING present ones, and renaming either half of a pair would have
// left the entry alive (the surviving twin kept marking it seen) to go on excusing a third occurrence.
// 439 is what the gate itself printed with the ledger emptied, so it is re-derivable in one command.

